00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:06.630
But if you just joined,
feel free to just raise

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:09.690
your hand on Zoom or you can
use the Zoom chat as well.

00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:14.880
But something I also
wanted to ask you about,

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:19.710
Emi, is in the Hangouts we
talk a lot about communication

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:21.280
across the business.

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:25.630
And I know this is something
that you focus a lot on,

00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:28.890
bridging the gap between data
science and the business.

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:32.280
And I'm curious if you have
any tips for all of us.

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:34.020
Yeah.

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:37.080
Big part of my role now, and
I'm fairly new in the role,

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:37.890
like three months.

00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:42.030
But even my previous role, I was
leading the advanced analytics

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:45.210
team, that was kind
of the translators

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:47.760
in between the business
and the data scientist.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:51.390
And even now, I'm as
a data intelligence,

00:10:51.390 --> 00:10:55.350
I'm in between sales
people and data scientists

00:10:55.350 --> 00:10:57.540
or business intelligence team.

00:10:57.540 --> 00:11:00.330
I think one thing that is
really, really important

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:03.510
is understanding the
business, understanding

00:11:03.510 --> 00:11:07.440
the people using the different
technology that we have.

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:09.845
And I will split it in two ways.

00:11:09.845 --> 00:11:12.210
So one is the leaders.

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:14.820
And the other one is the
frontline associate, so

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:16.770
people in the field.

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:19.290
So you need to understand both.

00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:23.040
You need to make sure that
both are agree with whatever

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:24.360
you plan to build.

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:26.220
And let me start
with the leader.

00:11:26.220 --> 00:11:29.040
So definitely, you need them.

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:31.590
Cannot ignore what they want.

00:11:31.590 --> 00:11:32.910
They know about the strategy.

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:34.800
They have the funding.

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:36.040
Money is very important.

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:39.040
So without money, it's very
hard to do anything else.

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:42.360
So make sure that
you understand where

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:44.890
they want to go, what
are you planning to do.

00:11:44.890 --> 00:11:46.680
What are the
challenges they face?

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:50.920
What are the things that
they want to achieve?

00:11:50.920 --> 00:11:56.370
And ask questions, and get
to know them personally.

00:11:56.370 --> 00:12:00.780
I think that helps, get
closer to their heart.

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:05.730
And but then explain what
you do and how it works.

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:09.810
But knowing that they
don't live in our world.

00:12:09.810 --> 00:12:14.915
And they don't want to learn
all the stuff that we know,

00:12:14.915 --> 00:12:16.290
because they don't
have the time,

00:12:16.290 --> 00:12:19.130
and their focus is to run the
business, not to understand

00:12:19.130 --> 00:12:21.090
AI and data analytics.

00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:24.420
But knowing what
is enough so they

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:26.700
can get a good
sense of what we do,

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:31.920
and they can relate
and think about how

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:35.820
that can be used for the work
that they want to be done.

00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:40.740
So again, not to
be a brown nose,

00:12:40.740 --> 00:12:43.710
like it's said of
them, but really

00:12:43.710 --> 00:12:47.580
important to know them and
get to understand them.

00:12:47.580 --> 00:12:51.870
And then, you have the frontline
people, like sale people,

00:12:51.870 --> 00:12:54.660
call center, whomever is
going to use the technology

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:55.830
that you have.

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:58.100
Make the things easier for them.

00:12:58.100 --> 00:12:59.850
Don't build something
then say, well, they

00:12:59.850 --> 00:13:01.650
need to figure
out how to use it.

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:03.150
Because they're not
going to use it.

00:13:03.150 --> 00:13:06.730
They are busy.

00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:08.040
They have a lot to do.

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:11.070
They have a very time
matrix, whatever else--

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:13.950
whatever that is,
like a sales goals,

00:13:13.950 --> 00:13:16.980
call they need to
handle, whatever

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:19.300
that might be the case.

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:26.130
So you need to make it very
easy so that doesn't drastically

00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:27.690
change the work they do.

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:29.490
Think about Netflix.

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:33.300
When you watch Netflix and
you have recommendations,

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:35.520
you just watch TV
and you just get

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:38.310
the different recommendations,
and you just select that.

00:13:38.310 --> 00:13:40.770
So you don't need to know
what is going on there,

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:43.680
or you don't need to
understand the algorithm,

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:46.740
or change the way you watch TV.

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:49.350
It's just a helpful tool.

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:55.920
So for our sale people,
we tend to make mistakes.

00:13:55.920 --> 00:13:57.850
We give them a lot
of information,

00:13:57.850 --> 00:13:59.890
a lot of data points,
a lot of dashboards,

00:13:59.890 --> 00:14:01.350
and we overwhelm them.

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:03.090
And then they get
to a point that they

00:14:03.090 --> 00:14:04.570
don't know where to find it.

00:14:04.570 --> 00:14:06.840
They don't have the
time to look at it.

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:10.350
They get-- there's
too many data points

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:15.570
that they get this phrase
with all these dashboards

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:17.860
and situations there.

00:14:17.860 --> 00:14:20.790
So we need to be,
instead of giving them

00:14:20.790 --> 00:14:24.600
a lot of dashboards and cool
visualizations and things

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:27.570
that they might not even look
at it, because, again, they

00:14:27.570 --> 00:14:30.660
get overwhelmed, tell
them what is the actual.

00:14:30.660 --> 00:14:32.550
Who they need to
contact and why.

00:14:32.550 --> 00:14:34.990
And tell them what to do next.

00:14:34.990 --> 00:14:38.080
So they start the day
and say, all right,

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:39.090
what do I need to do?

00:14:39.090 --> 00:14:41.610
OK, you've got to
call these people.

00:14:41.610 --> 00:14:43.410
And you're going
to talk about this.

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:48.270
And you are going to
then do these activities.

00:14:48.270 --> 00:14:54.140
And a little bit of why helps
in simple English definitely.

00:14:54.140 --> 00:14:57.480
Don't build in procedure,
and inaccuracy,

00:14:57.480 --> 00:15:00.630
and anything like that, or
don't share those things.

00:15:00.630 --> 00:15:03.390
But then, but you need
to build the trust.

00:15:03.390 --> 00:15:05.460
For example, things
that are ways

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:08.580
to erode the trust if you tell
them, hey, contact this person,

00:15:08.580 --> 00:15:10.050
and the phone number is wrong.

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:12.690
Or contact this
person and the person

00:15:12.690 --> 00:15:14.790
is no longer working
for that company.

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:18.300
Or hey, do this, and they
try it, and they try it,

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:20.170
and that doesn't work.

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:22.410
You're going to lose that trust.

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:24.880
And that's definitely not good.

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:27.675
So make sure your data is good.

00:15:27.675 --> 00:15:32.310
You tell, you validate some
of the things you are sharing.

00:15:32.310 --> 00:15:34.080
And ask for feedback.

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:37.410
People forgive if you give
them something that didn't work

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:40.830
or the data wasn't good, but if
you are arrogant and you say,

00:15:40.830 --> 00:15:42.000
but you got to use it.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:45.270
And I'm sure what
I gave you is good,

00:15:45.270 --> 00:15:48.550
they're just going to
ignore it in the future.

00:15:48.550 --> 00:15:51.630
So again, start
with your leaders.

00:15:51.630 --> 00:15:52.650
Understand them.

00:15:52.650 --> 00:15:56.430
Get closer to them
personally, professionally.

00:15:56.430 --> 00:16:03.750
And then, but don't lose
the focus on the end user.

00:16:06.570 --> 00:16:07.770
Thank you.

00:16:07.770 --> 00:16:11.330
And when you say don't
overwhelm them with dashboards,

00:16:11.330 --> 00:16:15.704
how are you serving that
information to them?

00:16:15.704 --> 00:16:19.680
So the dashboard, I see one
of the comments by dashboard.

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:24.090
Yeah, I think there's nothing
wrong about having dashboard.

00:16:24.090 --> 00:16:26.820
The problem is that
you need to share

00:16:26.820 --> 00:16:29.160
with them what is really
meaningful for the work

00:16:29.160 --> 00:16:29.910
that they do.

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:31.050
Keep it simple.

00:16:31.050 --> 00:16:32.580
And if they want
to get more detail,

00:16:32.580 --> 00:16:36.550
probably have a way that
they get drill into details.

00:16:36.550 --> 00:16:40.320
And be specific on
what they need to do.

00:16:40.320 --> 00:16:43.750
If you say, hey,
here is a dashboard.

00:16:43.750 --> 00:16:47.250
And you can see how much they
sell, and the opportunities,

00:16:47.250 --> 00:16:48.990
and what can be next.

00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:51.500
But be very clear
of what is the--

00:16:51.500 --> 00:16:54.360
you are asking for the
ask, or the expectation.

00:16:54.360 --> 00:16:57.360
Hey, you want to start
talking from here to here.

00:16:57.360 --> 00:16:58.500
And this is why.

00:16:58.500 --> 00:17:00.810
And this is some data
points that might help you

00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:02.160
with your conversation.

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:05.054
So keep it simple like that.

00:17:05.054 --> 00:17:07.980
And if they want to
explore more, and give them

00:17:07.980 --> 00:17:10.619
the opportunity to explore more.

00:17:10.619 --> 00:17:13.540
So people still like to get that
in Excel, and play with that.

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:15.690
So it's OK.

00:17:15.690 --> 00:17:25.560
But be specific, and don't
make it to a black box

00:17:25.560 --> 00:17:31.060
that they don't understand
what's going on and why.

00:17:31.060 --> 00:17:33.300
Marlene, I see you
asked a great question

00:17:33.300 --> 00:17:34.690
in the chat a bit earlier.

00:17:34.690 --> 00:17:35.650
Do you want to jump in?

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:39.880
I actually think--

00:17:39.880 --> 00:17:42.350
I mean, and you actually
answered it already.

00:17:42.350 --> 00:17:43.900
But I was just curious--

00:17:43.900 --> 00:17:51.550
really, I struggle with the
c-suite in my organization.

00:17:51.550 --> 00:17:53.800
I can give them
flashy, because flashy

00:17:53.800 --> 00:17:56.140
feels like it's new age,
and bright and shiny,

00:17:56.140 --> 00:17:59.080
and look at what the 21st
century can bring you.

00:17:59.080 --> 00:18:02.200
But you try to drill
in, here's the take-away

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:04.480
from what you're looking at.

00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:07.150
The only problem is,
I have severe anxiety

00:18:07.150 --> 00:18:08.210
about live demos.

00:18:08.210 --> 00:18:12.190
So I was just curious, do
you prefer live demos maybe?

00:18:12.190 --> 00:18:14.692
Or that question was way
more open-ended than what

00:18:14.692 --> 00:18:15.400
I'm getting into.

00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:16.600
But that was the idea.

00:18:16.600 --> 00:18:21.430
Something vivid and
moving, or do you

00:18:21.430 --> 00:18:23.770
prefer static and something
that they could just

00:18:23.770 --> 00:18:26.650
stare at and take in,
and really get everything

00:18:26.650 --> 00:18:27.880
that's on the screen?

00:18:27.880 --> 00:18:28.750
Yeah.

00:18:28.750 --> 00:18:30.760
And great question.

00:18:30.760 --> 00:18:33.580
And I don't think
there's a magic formula.

00:18:33.580 --> 00:18:35.950
But I will tell you
that the higher you

00:18:35.950 --> 00:18:39.340
go in the organization, the more
you behave like a little kid.

00:18:39.340 --> 00:18:42.010
The attention span
is very limited.

00:18:42.010 --> 00:18:43.960
They have so many
things going on,

00:18:43.960 --> 00:18:47.050
so much stuff that
they need to handle,

00:18:47.050 --> 00:18:49.990
that if you put a
lot of things, or you

00:18:49.990 --> 00:18:53.470
try to demonstrate them very
complicated, you lose them,

00:18:53.470 --> 00:18:56.230
and it's very hard to get them
back-- their attention back

00:18:56.230 --> 00:18:58.070
to what you want them to do.

00:18:58.070 --> 00:19:00.550
So a little kid, you
play with the little kid,

00:19:00.550 --> 00:19:02.530
and they get bored,
and they move on.

00:19:02.530 --> 00:19:03.550
They do something else.

00:19:03.550 --> 00:19:05.800
And you say, no, no, no, but
here is this or that toy.

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:08.780
And they're like, ah,
they're on something else.

00:19:08.780 --> 00:19:12.430
So I think it's a little
bit behave like that.

00:19:12.430 --> 00:19:15.640
So you need to be, hey,
what they really care?

00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:17.260
What is in their mind?

00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:20.080
What is the things that
they are worried now?

00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:23.260
They want to increase sales
in a specific product.

00:19:23.260 --> 00:19:25.000
They want to reach a new market.

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:27.550
They want to target a
new channel, whatever

00:19:27.550 --> 00:19:28.360
that might be.

00:19:28.360 --> 00:19:31.420
Or they want to reduce number of
calls, whatever that might be.

00:19:31.420 --> 00:19:33.820
And be a-- start high-level.

00:19:33.820 --> 00:19:35.950
Like, hey, you want to do this.

00:19:35.950 --> 00:19:38.590
I see this in the data.

00:19:38.590 --> 00:19:44.200
And you follow, and maybe asking
questions, or look at them.

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:48.310
Like either it is in person,
now with the video conference,

00:19:48.310 --> 00:19:49.570
hopefully they are on camera.

00:19:49.570 --> 00:19:52.110
You can tell, are they nodding?

00:19:52.110 --> 00:19:53.680
Are they distracted?

00:19:53.680 --> 00:19:55.720
They go from camera,
you lost them.

00:19:55.720 --> 00:19:58.270
So be mindful about
reading the people,

00:19:58.270 --> 00:20:00.640
not only what they
said, but what

00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:02.270
they do with their expression.

00:20:02.270 --> 00:20:04.280
So keep an eye on that.

00:20:04.280 --> 00:20:07.580
And as you talk about, and
again, start high level.

00:20:07.580 --> 00:20:09.130
Don't worry about
the technology.

00:20:09.130 --> 00:20:10.600
Don't worry about the--

00:20:10.600 --> 00:20:11.800
don't talk about the tool.

00:20:11.800 --> 00:20:17.060
Don't talk about how you
made the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:20:17.060 --> 00:20:21.160
But keep it something
that they care.

00:20:21.160 --> 00:20:24.370
And I think, hopefully,
if you engage them,

00:20:24.370 --> 00:20:27.100
they're going to start asking
more specific questions.

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:30.250
Live demos, to your specific
question about live demos,

00:20:30.250 --> 00:20:32.360
live demos are dangerous.

00:20:32.360 --> 00:20:35.320
So if you have a--

00:20:35.320 --> 00:20:38.620
PowerPoints are still rolling
most of the companies.

00:20:38.620 --> 00:20:41.230
So if you get a screenshot,
and you put it there,

00:20:41.230 --> 00:20:45.010
and that's something
that you prove it works.

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:48.250
And know what you're
presenting very well.

00:20:48.250 --> 00:20:50.500
So get a very good example.

00:20:50.500 --> 00:20:52.120
Get a very good dashboard.

00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:54.550
Get to know the
dashboard very well.

00:20:54.550 --> 00:20:56.740
Because if they ask you
something about that,

00:20:56.740 --> 00:20:58.990
how you calculated this, or
how you come up with this,

00:20:58.990 --> 00:21:01.060
or tell me more
about that example,

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:02.620
you don't know that
very well, you're

00:21:02.620 --> 00:21:06.850
going to lose that
trust, and the perception

00:21:06.850 --> 00:21:08.950
will change about
the work that you do.

00:21:08.950 --> 00:21:14.950
And probably, lastly
I will say, be your--

00:21:14.950 --> 00:21:17.488
like the [INAUDIBLE].

00:21:17.488 --> 00:21:19.780
What are the things that
they're going to challenge me?

00:21:19.780 --> 00:21:22.363
What are the things that can go
wrong about this presentation?

00:21:22.363 --> 00:21:24.010
What are the things
that they might not

00:21:24.010 --> 00:21:27.933
like about what I'm presenting?

00:21:27.933 --> 00:21:29.600
I'm sure they're not
going to like this.

00:21:29.600 --> 00:21:32.300
How am I going to convince
them to like this?

00:21:32.300 --> 00:21:37.160
So don't prepare for the
best possible scenario.

00:21:37.160 --> 00:21:40.580
Prepare for the worst
scenario, and that

00:21:40.580 --> 00:21:43.910
will get you ready for
if some of that happened,

00:21:43.910 --> 00:21:45.780
or all of that happened.

00:21:45.780 --> 00:21:47.930
So I don't know if that helped.

00:21:47.930 --> 00:21:49.050
That was very helpful.

00:21:49.050 --> 00:21:50.120
Thank you so much.

00:21:50.120 --> 00:21:51.920
EMI ZAMBADA: Yeah.

00:21:51.920 --> 00:21:53.420
RACHAEL DEMPSEY:
Wanted to jump over

00:21:53.420 --> 00:21:56.150
to a few questions
that were on Slido.

00:21:56.150 --> 00:21:59.390
And so one was, what type
of data do you work with?

00:21:59.390 --> 00:22:02.270
Where does the data come from?

00:22:02.270 --> 00:22:05.000
And I guess this is a few
questions [LAUGHS] in one.

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:08.210
And also, what tools do your
300-plus people work with?

00:22:08.210 --> 00:22:12.990
Excel, Python, R,
SAS, in what mix?

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:13.950
Yeah.

00:22:13.950 --> 00:22:18.210
Like probably any other
big older organization,

00:22:18.210 --> 00:22:20.160
we're almost 100
years in business.

00:22:20.160 --> 00:22:24.010
So we have in different
business units.

00:22:24.010 --> 00:22:27.990
So we have so many data
sources, so many legacy systems.

00:22:27.990 --> 00:22:33.180
So data is all over
the place, different--

00:22:33.180 --> 00:22:35.640
excuse me-- different
quality, different shape,

00:22:35.640 --> 00:22:40.430
different format,
different databases.

00:22:40.430 --> 00:22:43.820
So definitely, it's a challenge.

00:22:43.820 --> 00:22:50.720
We try to consolidate and merit
a lot of those data points.

00:22:50.720 --> 00:22:51.950
We have data legs.

00:22:51.950 --> 00:22:57.510
We have data stores, and
data model, you name it.

00:22:57.510 --> 00:23:00.950
So I would like to say,
oh, yeah, we nailed it.

00:23:00.950 --> 00:23:04.350
We know all the data
thing, and it's perfect.

00:23:04.350 --> 00:23:07.610
So definitely, it's
a big challenge.

00:23:07.610 --> 00:23:10.580
Data engineers are
way underestimated--

00:23:10.580 --> 00:23:13.310
I think, that we
don't have many.

00:23:13.310 --> 00:23:17.090
We probably need
more and better.

00:23:17.090 --> 00:23:18.830
We have great people,
don't get me wrong.

00:23:18.830 --> 00:23:22.250
But definitely, it's a very,
very much needed profession.

00:23:22.250 --> 00:23:25.970
And if you are into data
engineering, you got it.

00:23:25.970 --> 00:23:28.200
That's a great place to be.

00:23:28.200 --> 00:23:33.530
We use a lot of structured
data from a honor system,

00:23:33.530 --> 00:23:36.890
like reports reports,
and transactional data,

00:23:36.890 --> 00:23:38.400
and things like that.

00:23:38.400 --> 00:23:41.730
But we also use a lot of
unstructured data, like calls,

00:23:41.730 --> 00:23:49.250
texts, emails, a lot
of third party data.

00:23:49.250 --> 00:23:53.300
We have quite a lot of
information about the market,

00:23:53.300 --> 00:23:54.620
about the customers.

00:23:54.620 --> 00:23:58.940
So definitely a lot
of temporary data,

00:23:58.940 --> 00:24:03.000
and trying to combine
with the internal data.

00:24:03.000 --> 00:24:05.930
So in terms of tools,
a lot of Python,

00:24:05.930 --> 00:24:08.630
R, still a lot of Excel.

00:24:08.630 --> 00:24:13.310
Probably something we are
evolving, but still very

00:24:13.310 --> 00:24:14.660
popular out there.

00:24:14.660 --> 00:24:16.880
A lot of Tableau.

00:24:16.880 --> 00:24:21.350
We started more with Power BI.

00:24:21.350 --> 00:24:24.630
In terms of databases,
there's Snowflakes, a lot of--

00:24:24.630 --> 00:24:32.990
we're big AWS job, so S3
bucket, and [INAUDIBLE] maker.

00:24:32.990 --> 00:24:37.340
So I tried to narrow
the number of tools

00:24:37.340 --> 00:24:40.850
that are used to make
it easier for the team,

00:24:40.850 --> 00:24:46.520
but still different data bricks,
different tools out there.

00:24:46.520 --> 00:24:51.770
We try to-- we're big into open
source tools like Python and R

00:24:51.770 --> 00:24:53.780
and things like that as part.

00:24:53.780 --> 00:24:56.540
But still, we use a lot
of commercial tools.

00:24:56.540 --> 00:25:01.400
We used to do a lot of
SAS, no longer a SAS job.

00:25:01.400 --> 00:25:02.330
But yeah.

00:25:02.330 --> 00:25:03.510
I hope that helped.

00:25:06.580 --> 00:25:08.590
Thank you.

00:25:08.590 --> 00:25:11.655
I see some feedback
in the chat, that--

00:25:11.655 --> 00:25:13.410
[LAUGHS] Javier
said, if Emi decides

00:25:13.410 --> 00:25:16.020
to be a career coach one
day for data scientists,

00:25:16.020 --> 00:25:18.656
I think he'd be very successful.

00:25:18.656 --> 00:25:21.690
[LAUGHS] Thank you, Javier.

00:25:21.690 --> 00:25:24.060
But of on that
topic, I know you

00:25:24.060 --> 00:25:28.230
have served as an advocate for
minority groups in AI and tech.

00:25:28.230 --> 00:25:30.690
And I was curious
if that is something

00:25:30.690 --> 00:25:35.760
that you do for people to
help bring them into the space

00:25:35.760 --> 00:25:37.150
as well.

00:25:37.150 --> 00:25:39.990
And if you have maybe
some ideas for all of us

00:25:39.990 --> 00:25:43.180
about how we could
get more involved too.

00:25:43.180 --> 00:25:43.810
Yeah.

00:25:43.810 --> 00:25:48.460
I think it's, diversity is
very important in any space.

00:25:48.460 --> 00:25:52.420
But data scientist is
definitely super important

00:25:52.420 --> 00:25:54.850
because we build the
future for companies.

00:25:54.850 --> 00:25:57.190
Where we build the
new capabilities.

00:25:57.190 --> 00:26:01.870
We are truly the future of how
we're going to do business.

00:26:01.870 --> 00:26:06.100
And by diversity, it doesn't
only means a color, race,

00:26:06.100 --> 00:26:07.360
ethnicity, gender.

00:26:07.360 --> 00:26:10.330
It can also be
diversity of background,

00:26:10.330 --> 00:26:17.420
experiences, expertises that
you bring to the company.

00:26:17.420 --> 00:26:23.770
So we have the chief
advocate for the Latino group

00:26:23.770 --> 00:26:25.610
here at Nationwide.

00:26:25.610 --> 00:26:29.410
And our executive
sponsor is the president

00:26:29.410 --> 00:26:32.950
of the one of the
biggest business unit.

00:26:32.950 --> 00:26:35.440
And he said, diversity is
not only the right thing

00:26:35.440 --> 00:26:38.260
to do, but a good
business decision.

00:26:38.260 --> 00:26:42.070
And what that means is, you
think about the demographic

00:26:42.070 --> 00:26:44.682
in US.

00:26:44.682 --> 00:26:46.390
I might get this a
little slightly wrong.

00:26:46.390 --> 00:26:49.870
But 60% of the
population are Black

00:26:49.870 --> 00:26:53.890
and they have a $1.8
trillion purchasing power.

00:26:53.890 --> 00:26:58.030
And Latinos are close to
20% with another close to $2

00:26:58.030 --> 00:26:59.800
trillion purchasing power.

00:26:59.800 --> 00:27:03.820
And you think about a
woman, half the population,

00:27:03.820 --> 00:27:08.770
a big group are growing and
getting more into business

00:27:08.770 --> 00:27:10.730
and increasing the
purchasing power.

00:27:10.730 --> 00:27:14.440
So it's a lot of money
there that if you

00:27:14.440 --> 00:27:17.080
don't tap into that, if
you don't build solutions,

00:27:17.080 --> 00:27:20.710
products for those individual--
and they think different.

00:27:20.710 --> 00:27:22.520
There's no right or wrong.

00:27:22.520 --> 00:27:24.550
But every group they have
their specific needs,

00:27:24.550 --> 00:27:26.800
their specific characteristic.

00:27:26.800 --> 00:27:28.510
And you need to
understand those so you

00:27:28.510 --> 00:27:31.000
can tailor your product,
your solution for them,

00:27:31.000 --> 00:27:35.170
and they can-- and they feel
good about buying your product

00:27:35.170 --> 00:27:37.490
because they feel like
you care about them.

00:27:37.490 --> 00:27:39.820
You are socially responsible.

00:27:39.820 --> 00:27:42.370
And you support their groups.

00:27:42.370 --> 00:27:45.790
And there's no other way
to build those products

00:27:45.790 --> 00:27:49.880
than having the people that
understand those individual.

00:27:49.880 --> 00:27:52.960
So if you are a data
scientist, if you

00:27:52.960 --> 00:27:57.100
don't have think about a group
that doesn't have any minority

00:27:57.100 --> 00:28:00.340
groups, white males, all--

00:28:00.340 --> 00:28:02.590
nothing bad, white
males are amazing.

00:28:02.590 --> 00:28:05.957
But think about, there's
no diversity at all.

00:28:05.957 --> 00:28:07.540
So those individual,
they're not going

00:28:07.540 --> 00:28:12.670
to know exactly how women
think, or Latinos, or Black,

00:28:12.670 --> 00:28:16.450
and it's going to be very hard
for them to understand nuances

00:28:16.450 --> 00:28:19.730
and specific things that they
are very important for them.

00:28:19.730 --> 00:28:22.930
And they're not going
to understand that maybe

00:28:22.930 --> 00:28:25.840
language is critical.

00:28:25.840 --> 00:28:27.430
Something Hispanic,
they're not going

00:28:27.430 --> 00:28:30.880
to know that probably data
is biased or some of them.

00:28:30.880 --> 00:28:34.330
People building computer
vision system that

00:28:34.330 --> 00:28:39.070
doesn't use a balanced data
set and doesn't correctly

00:28:39.070 --> 00:28:43.137
train the models to identify
properly Black people,

00:28:43.137 --> 00:28:44.470
they're going to have a problem.

00:28:44.470 --> 00:28:46.810
Or people doing
speech recognition,

00:28:46.810 --> 00:28:50.200
building the system to
transcribe as patient to text

00:28:50.200 --> 00:28:52.540
and doing a lot of
solution based on that.

00:28:52.540 --> 00:28:54.610
If they don't
understand that people

00:28:54.610 --> 00:28:57.670
might have a different accent,
different way of talking,

00:28:57.670 --> 00:28:59.860
they're not going
to potentially use

00:28:59.860 --> 00:29:02.260
the right data sets to
train the models right,

00:29:02.260 --> 00:29:09.300
and therefore not working
properly for those groups.

00:29:09.300 --> 00:29:11.050
And those groups are
going to notice that.

00:29:11.050 --> 00:29:12.883
And they're not going
to be happy with that.

00:29:12.883 --> 00:29:14.960
And they're not going
to buy your products.

00:29:14.960 --> 00:29:18.400
There was a public
cases about that.

00:29:18.400 --> 00:29:22.870
There's a lot of with
recruiting with Amazon

00:29:22.870 --> 00:29:25.090
and another insurance
company with claim.

00:29:25.090 --> 00:29:28.360
When you needed to record
a video about your claim

00:29:28.360 --> 00:29:29.570
and what happened.

00:29:29.570 --> 00:29:33.730
And it was biased, or
some groups, and people

00:29:33.730 --> 00:29:34.480
didn't like that.

00:29:34.480 --> 00:29:37.610
And they're going to--
people are very sensitive,

00:29:37.610 --> 00:29:40.810
and they demand a lot,
especially after COVID.

00:29:40.810 --> 00:29:43.880
So if you don't
treat them right,

00:29:43.880 --> 00:29:46.210
they are going to--
and with social media,

00:29:46.210 --> 00:29:49.820
things get shared very quickly.

00:29:49.820 --> 00:29:51.610
So if you don't
treat them right,

00:29:51.610 --> 00:29:53.300
they are not going
to buy your products.

00:29:53.300 --> 00:29:56.240
And they're going
to switch very fast.

00:29:56.240 --> 00:30:00.520
So again, diversity
helps with all of that.

00:30:00.520 --> 00:30:02.590
And how you can help?

00:30:02.590 --> 00:30:03.970
Be an ally.

00:30:03.970 --> 00:30:08.920
If you are a leader,
hire those individuals.

00:30:08.920 --> 00:30:11.830
You are going to have more
powerful better teams.

00:30:11.830 --> 00:30:14.770
If you are an
individual contributor,

00:30:14.770 --> 00:30:18.940
ask people, mentor
them, and help them.

00:30:18.940 --> 00:30:20.260
And you will learn too.

00:30:20.260 --> 00:30:25.480
You will learn a lot
from those interactions.

00:30:25.480 --> 00:30:27.230
Thank you, Emi.

00:30:27.230 --> 00:30:29.650
Andey, I see you asked
a question in the chat

00:30:29.650 --> 00:30:30.610
a little bit earlier.

00:30:30.610 --> 00:30:32.480
Do you want to jump in next?

00:30:32.480 --> 00:30:32.980
Sure.

00:30:32.980 --> 00:30:34.870
Thanks, Rachael.

00:30:34.870 --> 00:30:38.980
Emi, can you share about
the open source R adoption

00:30:38.980 --> 00:30:40.090
path for your team?

00:30:40.090 --> 00:30:41.660
I have a few questions here.

00:30:41.660 --> 00:30:45.280
So that open source R adoption.

00:30:45.280 --> 00:30:49.720
And did you get any
resistance from IT

00:30:49.720 --> 00:30:52.190
in supporting getting
R into production?

00:30:52.190 --> 00:30:55.210
And if so, what
lessons can you share

00:30:55.210 --> 00:30:56.950
about onboarding
and supporting folks

00:30:56.950 --> 00:30:59.530
new to R and open source tools?

00:30:59.530 --> 00:31:01.450
Yeah.

00:31:01.450 --> 00:31:04.630
It's definitely, of
course, security and data

00:31:04.630 --> 00:31:06.080
protection is important.

00:31:06.080 --> 00:31:10.720
So when we have this
dedicated environment,

00:31:10.720 --> 00:31:16.420
we call it model factory,
that is protected,

00:31:16.420 --> 00:31:18.430
it has all the
security controls,

00:31:18.430 --> 00:31:22.120
and we have R and Python
embedded into that.

00:31:22.120 --> 00:31:24.940
And every time
there's a new package,

00:31:24.940 --> 00:31:27.040
they get tested, the versions.

00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:31.120
You cannot go and get the latest
batch or any versions that you

00:31:31.120 --> 00:31:31.810
think is best.

00:31:31.810 --> 00:31:37.120
We have an approved version
for each of those tools.

00:31:37.120 --> 00:31:39.190
We, again, we test the packages.

00:31:39.190 --> 00:31:41.170
We give our very--

00:31:41.170 --> 00:31:43.590
we have very long--

00:31:43.590 --> 00:31:46.480
a lengthy procedures of
how to use it, what to do,

00:31:46.480 --> 00:31:47.320
what not to do.

00:31:47.320 --> 00:31:51.850
And we do peer review,
when people use, again,

00:31:51.850 --> 00:31:54.650
whatever package or
whatever code you write,

00:31:54.650 --> 00:31:58.480
that it gets a review
by one of your peers.

00:31:58.480 --> 00:32:01.360
And then, before
move to production,

00:32:01.360 --> 00:32:04.450
there is another team that
implement that and validates

00:32:04.450 --> 00:32:09.220
that you are not incurring
to any security issues,

00:32:09.220 --> 00:32:12.570
like a credential or
managing a specific way,

00:32:12.570 --> 00:32:15.100
you need to follow
that protocol.

00:32:15.100 --> 00:32:17.630
There's a lot of things
I cannot share publicly.

00:32:17.630 --> 00:32:23.430
But there's a lot of controls
around open source systems.

00:32:23.430 --> 00:32:27.320
So I think, again,
we have a lot of--

00:32:27.320 --> 00:32:28.520
we are big fans.

00:32:28.520 --> 00:32:29.540
It's cheaper.

00:32:29.540 --> 00:32:33.140
It's easier to come get the
talent knowing those tools,

00:32:33.140 --> 00:32:36.140
and people would like
to learn those tools.

00:32:36.140 --> 00:32:41.000
But definitely a lot of
controls to protect the data

00:32:41.000 --> 00:32:43.070
and protect the code.

00:32:43.070 --> 00:32:45.270
So I'm hearing,
guardrails are important.

00:32:45.270 --> 00:32:48.020
And then, just peer support?

00:32:48.020 --> 00:32:48.720
Yeah.

00:32:48.980 --> 00:32:50.313
ANDEY NUNES-BREWSTER: Thank you.

00:32:52.400 --> 00:32:53.030
Thank you.

00:32:53.030 --> 00:32:55.432
Alan, I see you also just
asked a question in the chat.

00:32:55.432 --> 00:32:56.390
Do you want to jump in?

00:33:00.140 --> 00:33:00.890
Yeah.

00:33:00.890 --> 00:33:03.470
I had to walk back
over to the computer.

00:33:03.470 --> 00:33:07.460
I'm pacing around in my office
because it's cold in here.

00:33:07.460 --> 00:33:10.280
I'm just curious, Emi, as you
describe this great big team

00:33:10.280 --> 00:33:12.980
of hundreds of people,
what kind of coordination

00:33:12.980 --> 00:33:14.840
happens across those teams?

00:33:14.840 --> 00:33:19.970
And is that a challenge for
leaders of various teams

00:33:19.970 --> 00:33:22.550
to figure out how
to collaborate?

00:33:22.550 --> 00:33:25.340
Do you have to have agreement
about things like architecture

00:33:25.340 --> 00:33:28.190
or about practices and
processes and stuff?

00:33:28.190 --> 00:33:31.880
Just wondering how you organize
across all of those people,

00:33:31.880 --> 00:33:34.070
and to what extent
that's even critical,

00:33:34.070 --> 00:33:36.520
or if they're
pretty independent?

00:33:36.520 --> 00:33:38.530
Yeah.

00:33:38.530 --> 00:33:41.210
Again, like any other
big organization,

00:33:41.210 --> 00:33:43.840
there's different groups
and different part

00:33:43.840 --> 00:33:47.140
of the different organization
across the company.

00:33:47.140 --> 00:33:49.570
it's like our data
scientists are in finance,

00:33:49.570 --> 00:33:51.640
or data people
are in technology.

00:33:51.640 --> 00:33:57.940
You have the data architecture
team, and the security team,

00:33:57.940 --> 00:34:01.030
and within-- even within
IT, or within finance,

00:34:01.030 --> 00:34:03.970
you have different groups
that are in business.

00:34:03.970 --> 00:34:07.450
So I'm not going to--

00:34:07.450 --> 00:34:08.330
I don't want to lie.

00:34:08.330 --> 00:34:11.630
And I don't want to ignore that
having different organizations,

00:34:11.630 --> 00:34:15.880
different leaders creates
some difficulties.

00:34:15.880 --> 00:34:18.820
We try to build a pot--

00:34:18.820 --> 00:34:19.940
group of people.

00:34:19.940 --> 00:34:22.630
So we want to make sure
that there is leadership

00:34:22.630 --> 00:34:24.550
support for the project.

00:34:24.550 --> 00:34:27.280
You cannot start without that.

00:34:27.280 --> 00:34:33.520
There can be some [INAUDIBLE]
that gets done more naturally.

00:34:33.520 --> 00:34:35.260
But you have a project.

00:34:35.260 --> 00:34:37.090
You have the support.

00:34:37.090 --> 00:34:39.130
You create your business case.

00:34:39.130 --> 00:34:40.330
You get the funding.

00:34:40.330 --> 00:34:43.886
And then you say, OK,
in order to do this,

00:34:43.886 --> 00:34:45.969
I will need the data
scientist, the data engineer.

00:34:45.969 --> 00:34:49.420
So a lot of this, you can--

00:34:49.420 --> 00:34:51.469
you have support,
you have the funding.

00:34:51.469 --> 00:34:55.210
But also say relationship
and building a network

00:34:55.210 --> 00:34:55.929
is important.

00:34:55.929 --> 00:34:59.890
Because you need to go with
those leaders and get buy-in.

00:34:59.890 --> 00:35:01.870
I even though, I might hear--

00:35:01.870 --> 00:35:04.540
I might say, hey,
my sale leader,

00:35:04.540 --> 00:35:07.660
the head of sales
for my business unit,

00:35:07.660 --> 00:35:09.290
it really wants to do this.

00:35:09.290 --> 00:35:13.180
But if I go I say, you got to
help me because I'm telling you

00:35:13.180 --> 00:35:14.630
I say so.

00:35:14.630 --> 00:35:18.320
That commitment might
not feel that strong.

00:35:18.320 --> 00:35:20.180
So you need to go with
them and say, hey,

00:35:20.180 --> 00:35:21.880
we're doing this
because of this.

00:35:21.880 --> 00:35:24.430
And what do you
think about that?

00:35:24.430 --> 00:35:27.400
And yeah, you might
need to find the team

00:35:27.400 --> 00:35:31.370
or get some resources for that.

00:35:31.370 --> 00:35:34.510
But then that
convincing is important,

00:35:34.510 --> 00:35:35.990
the why is important.

00:35:35.990 --> 00:35:38.770
And then you go to this
other group and do the same.

00:35:38.770 --> 00:35:40.390
And all you have these people--

00:35:40.390 --> 00:35:44.170
one thing I like to do is--

00:35:44.170 --> 00:35:45.910
I'm not going to
tell them what to do.

00:35:45.910 --> 00:35:48.400
I'm going to-- I have
this philosophy that you

00:35:48.400 --> 00:35:52.720
need to be very tied on what
is the outcome that you want,

00:35:52.720 --> 00:35:57.560
what is the objective, and
what is that it will be needed.

00:35:57.560 --> 00:35:58.870
So be very clear on that.

00:35:58.870 --> 00:36:01.120
And hopefully they
truly understand that.

00:36:01.120 --> 00:36:06.150
And you describe it but then you
wait for them to describe it.

00:36:06.150 --> 00:36:10.070
And you reiterate until
it kind of sound--

00:36:10.070 --> 00:36:11.233
it's clear to them.

00:36:11.233 --> 00:36:12.400
I think that's the best way.

00:36:12.400 --> 00:36:14.960
Repeat what we're trying to do.

00:36:14.960 --> 00:36:17.140
And if they are still
loose or not very clear,

00:36:17.140 --> 00:36:20.320
you got to keep that message
until they are clear.

00:36:20.320 --> 00:36:21.650
So be tight on that.

00:36:21.650 --> 00:36:23.140
But then be loose on that--

00:36:23.140 --> 00:36:24.385
how they get there.

00:36:24.385 --> 00:36:25.870
They are way smarter than me.

00:36:25.870 --> 00:36:28.480
So I will never tell
them, use this algorithm,

00:36:28.480 --> 00:36:33.460
or do it this way, because
that controlling is not

00:36:33.460 --> 00:36:36.310
going to-- it will
inhibit innovation.

00:36:36.310 --> 00:36:38.320
And they are expert.

00:36:38.320 --> 00:36:39.820
So we loose on that.

00:36:39.820 --> 00:36:45.610
Let them define what is done,
what is the way, the how.

00:36:45.610 --> 00:36:49.141
The why and what is
important to be tight.

00:36:49.141 --> 00:36:50.890
The how, they figure it out.

00:36:50.890 --> 00:36:52.180
They are smart.

00:36:52.180 --> 00:36:56.245
But then, be tight on the
result. Go back and be tight on

00:36:56.245 --> 00:36:59.320
are we achieving what we want?

00:36:59.320 --> 00:37:04.660
And be very, very specific
and simple in that metric.

00:37:04.660 --> 00:37:07.570
I hear someone or saw
someone talking about KPI.

00:37:07.570 --> 00:37:09.640
So I want to increase sales.

00:37:09.640 --> 00:37:12.910
I want to reach
this market share.

00:37:12.910 --> 00:37:16.490
I want to-- and make
that metric that

00:37:16.490 --> 00:37:19.090
is simple to understand
for the data scientists

00:37:19.090 --> 00:37:21.470
how to get there, but
simple for the business

00:37:21.470 --> 00:37:25.330
to understand that we're
achieving what we committed

00:37:25.330 --> 00:37:28.660
to do or committed to achieve.

00:37:28.660 --> 00:37:34.310
So that, I think,
that's important for us.

00:37:34.310 --> 00:37:38.260
So tight, loose, tight,
give them the money,

00:37:38.260 --> 00:37:39.910
give them the
resources, give them

00:37:39.910 --> 00:37:43.270
the people that is needed
to let them run with that.

00:37:43.270 --> 00:37:48.100
So we started getting very big
into the project to product,

00:37:48.100 --> 00:37:50.770
not being very specific
on the projects

00:37:50.770 --> 00:37:53.230
and how all the waterfall
and all that, but

00:37:53.230 --> 00:37:55.420
be more of the product.

00:37:55.420 --> 00:37:58.360
Here is the product that we're
trying to get out of this.

00:37:58.360 --> 00:38:02.290
And let them figure
out the details

00:38:02.290 --> 00:38:04.030
of how to get to that product.

00:38:04.030 --> 00:38:08.385
And use the money and
resources as they wish.

00:38:08.385 --> 00:38:09.010
That's great.

00:38:09.010 --> 00:38:09.510
Thank you.

00:38:09.510 --> 00:38:12.970
I really like that description
of the articulation of where

00:38:12.970 --> 00:38:16.120
to be really tight and focused
versus where to let people run.

00:38:16.120 --> 00:38:18.250
And that can be really,
really successful

00:38:18.250 --> 00:38:20.890
when you've got skilled
people and platforms to work

00:38:20.890 --> 00:38:22.840
from that they can use well.

00:38:22.840 --> 00:38:26.207
And you can let them
be successful in ways

00:38:26.207 --> 00:38:27.790
that they're really,
really strong at,

00:38:27.790 --> 00:38:30.340
and know that you're going to
get to a well defined outcome

00:38:30.340 --> 00:38:32.200
that you're helping steer.

00:38:32.200 --> 00:38:33.700
Yeah, I appreciate
hearing all that.

00:38:33.700 --> 00:38:34.240
Thank you.

00:38:34.240 --> 00:38:35.260
No, thank you.

00:38:35.260 --> 00:38:38.530
Thanks, Alan.

00:38:38.530 --> 00:38:44.550
I see a great conversation
in the chat around metrics,

00:38:44.550 --> 00:38:47.640
around dashboards, a number
of things to show in them.

00:38:47.640 --> 00:38:52.260
And I think it's Xinyi,
and sorry if I pronounced

00:38:52.260 --> 00:38:53.520
your name incorrectly.

00:38:53.520 --> 00:38:57.980
Would you want to jump in and
ask the question you put there?

00:38:57.980 --> 00:38:59.120
Yeah.

00:38:59.120 --> 00:39:02.060
Thank you.

00:39:02.060 --> 00:39:05.090
Actually, the
question is about how

00:39:05.090 --> 00:39:08.570
to build a story
for the C-suite.

00:39:08.570 --> 00:39:11.600
Because actually, we
are building dashboards

00:39:11.600 --> 00:39:16.640
that are a management system.

00:39:16.640 --> 00:39:22.520
So the dashboard
should be fixed just

00:39:22.520 --> 00:39:27.620
for a bunch of metrics
on that dashboard.

00:39:27.620 --> 00:39:34.280
But when we make plan,
design the dashboard as well,

00:39:34.280 --> 00:39:40.370
if we only put several,
maybe four or five metrics

00:39:40.370 --> 00:39:45.440
on the top and the
table of quick contacts,

00:39:45.440 --> 00:39:49.040
this might not be
enough for a big company

00:39:49.040 --> 00:39:51.770
since the story might be fixed.

00:39:51.770 --> 00:39:55.530
But sometimes, our
C-suite might want

00:39:55.530 --> 00:39:59.400
to know other side,
or another story.

00:39:59.400 --> 00:40:07.010
So we are thinking about to
put maybe 20 or more index

00:40:07.010 --> 00:40:08.390
on that dashboard.

00:40:08.390 --> 00:40:15.050
But, of course, the C-suite
will say, oh, it's too much.

00:40:15.050 --> 00:40:24.320
We do not have time to
read all that long reports.

00:40:24.320 --> 00:40:25.795
Yeah.

00:40:25.795 --> 00:40:26.420
Great question.

00:40:26.420 --> 00:40:30.230
And definitely,
storytelling is critical.

00:40:30.230 --> 00:40:33.530
I go back to the little kids.

00:40:33.530 --> 00:40:36.830
And you need to
speak their language.

00:40:36.830 --> 00:40:38.630
They are not going to
speak your language.

00:40:38.630 --> 00:40:39.740
Go back to the kids.

00:40:39.740 --> 00:40:42.060
You need to talk to the
kid in their language.

00:40:42.060 --> 00:40:45.620
You talk about the
Shakespearean language to a kid,

00:40:45.620 --> 00:40:48.470
they're not going to understand
you, and they don't care.

00:40:48.470 --> 00:40:50.220
You're going to lose them.

00:40:50.220 --> 00:40:52.400
So listen to your leaders.

00:40:52.400 --> 00:40:56.720
Listen to the earning
calls or whatever ways

00:40:56.720 --> 00:40:58.940
that they present
to the organization.

00:40:58.940 --> 00:41:00.980
Or whomever is the
leader, try to listen

00:41:00.980 --> 00:41:02.360
to previous conversation.

00:41:02.360 --> 00:41:04.640
And pay attention to
the words they use.

00:41:04.640 --> 00:41:06.920
Pay attention how
they describe things.

00:41:06.920 --> 00:41:09.860
Pay attention to the things
that they-- the metrics

00:41:09.860 --> 00:41:13.640
that they share, or the metrics
that they talk about it.

00:41:13.640 --> 00:41:15.320
And use those words.

00:41:15.320 --> 00:41:16.940
Words are important.

00:41:16.940 --> 00:41:19.580
You can say the same thing
with different words.

00:41:19.580 --> 00:41:23.660
Or you might have the way
you describe something,

00:41:23.660 --> 00:41:25.320
use their words.

00:41:25.320 --> 00:41:30.020
So if you can talk about-- we
talk about the data foundation.

00:41:30.020 --> 00:41:33.140
And but if you say
data integrity,

00:41:33.140 --> 00:41:34.640
you might say, well,
data integrity,

00:41:34.640 --> 00:41:36.150
data foundation is the same.

00:41:36.150 --> 00:41:38.420
But if they use the
word data foundation,

00:41:38.420 --> 00:41:41.360
use the same data foundation.

00:41:41.360 --> 00:41:45.050
If they say sales intelligence,
you can use another word.

00:41:45.050 --> 00:41:48.800
Use their words so
they can understand you

00:41:48.800 --> 00:41:52.061
and keep it consistent.

00:41:52.061 --> 00:41:57.590
If you keep changing the
words or the way they build.

00:41:57.590 --> 00:42:03.230
Think about in the old
times or even now Google,

00:42:03.230 --> 00:42:06.200
but think about
[INAUDIBLE] newspaper,

00:42:06.200 --> 00:42:08.090
or the format, any website.

00:42:08.090 --> 00:42:11.960
But think about the old
fashioned newspaper.

00:42:11.960 --> 00:42:14.070
New York Times, they
always look the same.

00:42:14.070 --> 00:42:17.900
They have the same structure,
the same format, the articles,

00:42:17.900 --> 00:42:20.130
the layout is similar.

00:42:20.130 --> 00:42:22.850
So every day you go
and read that newspaper

00:42:22.850 --> 00:42:26.640
or your online news, it
looks the same thing.

00:42:26.640 --> 00:42:29.730
Imagine if they will tweak
things and move things around,

00:42:29.730 --> 00:42:32.240
you will spend a lot
of energy and time

00:42:32.240 --> 00:42:33.420
trying to figure it out.

00:42:33.420 --> 00:42:37.040
And that mental
thing that your brain

00:42:37.040 --> 00:42:41.120
is taking energy to figuring out
the format and the structure.

00:42:41.120 --> 00:42:42.857
And you don't care about that.

00:42:42.857 --> 00:42:43.940
You want to read the news.

00:42:43.940 --> 00:42:46.610
So New York Times,
Washington Post, they

00:42:46.610 --> 00:42:47.960
keep the same structure.

00:42:47.960 --> 00:42:49.320
They keep it the same.

00:42:49.320 --> 00:42:51.530
So all your energy
is to understand

00:42:51.530 --> 00:42:54.410
the content of the news
that you're trying to read.

00:42:54.410 --> 00:42:57.650
So the same thing with the
storytelling and the dashboard.

00:42:57.650 --> 00:43:03.440
Find a way that the words,
the structure, the metrics

00:43:03.440 --> 00:43:05.910
that they use and
they're familiar with.

00:43:05.910 --> 00:43:08.330
So they don't spend
time and energy

00:43:08.330 --> 00:43:12.470
trying to understand your
dashboard, your story.

00:43:12.470 --> 00:43:14.338
That's something
they are familiar.

00:43:14.338 --> 00:43:16.130
The only thing they're
trying to understand

00:43:16.130 --> 00:43:19.130
is the story you are telling.

00:43:19.130 --> 00:43:21.800
The metric that you're
tracking is going this way,

00:43:21.800 --> 00:43:23.150
is going this other way.

00:43:23.150 --> 00:43:25.850
We might need to
take action on this.

00:43:25.850 --> 00:43:27.270
And be very specific.

00:43:30.920 --> 00:43:32.120
Don't say a lot of things.

00:43:32.120 --> 00:43:34.010
Less is better.

00:43:34.010 --> 00:43:35.820
Be clear and concise.

00:43:35.820 --> 00:43:41.710
But be very specific on the
things that you really care.

00:43:41.710 --> 00:43:42.570
Repeat.

00:43:42.570 --> 00:43:46.220
Repeat throughout your session
and throughout your dashboard.

00:43:46.220 --> 00:43:50.510
And then, and be very
specific about the action

00:43:50.510 --> 00:43:53.180
plan, what you need about them.

00:43:53.180 --> 00:44:01.290
And silence and
doesn't means agree.

00:44:01.290 --> 00:44:03.450
Silence can be, I
acknowledge that.

00:44:03.450 --> 00:44:05.760
But doesn't mean
that they agree.

00:44:05.760 --> 00:44:10.350
So watch them, look
at their expression.

00:44:10.350 --> 00:44:13.920
And next time you go, repeat.

00:44:13.920 --> 00:44:16.440
And the one thing that we--

00:44:16.440 --> 00:44:19.635
another mistake we make
is that we go back.

00:44:19.635 --> 00:44:21.510
Let's say you presented
something last month,

00:44:21.510 --> 00:44:24.090
and you're going this month
to present the same thing.

00:44:24.090 --> 00:44:27.390
Recap and say, last
month we talk about this.

00:44:27.390 --> 00:44:28.920
Now this is happening.

00:44:28.920 --> 00:44:33.280
And now I need you to know
this or do this for us.

00:44:33.280 --> 00:44:34.350
We need your support.

00:44:34.350 --> 00:44:39.150
And don't go there thinking that
they remember everything that

00:44:39.150 --> 00:44:41.220
happened last month,
they know everything

00:44:41.220 --> 00:44:45.210
that is happening in your world,
and assuming they know that.

00:44:45.210 --> 00:44:46.830
Because they don't.

00:44:46.830 --> 00:44:49.350
Whatever happened last month,
they did so many things--

00:44:49.350 --> 00:44:50.340
like the kid.

00:44:50.340 --> 00:44:52.500
So many things has
happened in their life

00:44:52.500 --> 00:44:54.420
that they are not
going to remember

00:44:54.420 --> 00:44:55.890
what you talk about last time.

00:44:55.890 --> 00:44:57.300
Don't feel offended.

00:44:57.300 --> 00:44:58.530
It's normal.

00:44:58.530 --> 00:45:02.460
Go back to that and say, last
time we spoke we did this.

00:45:02.460 --> 00:45:03.690
Now we're doing this.

00:45:03.690 --> 00:45:05.850
Now let's bring
it to the present.

00:45:08.580 --> 00:45:09.080
Yes.

00:45:09.080 --> 00:45:10.020
Thank you.

00:45:10.020 --> 00:45:10.520
Yeah.

00:45:10.520 --> 00:45:15.720
We need to do some
recaps in the system.

00:45:15.720 --> 00:45:17.180
Thank you so much.

00:45:17.180 --> 00:45:19.840
Thank you.

00:45:19.840 --> 00:45:20.680
Thank you, Emi.

00:45:20.680 --> 00:45:21.910
I love that.

00:45:21.910 --> 00:45:23.800
I have a tendency
to think, oh, we're

00:45:23.800 --> 00:45:26.800
going to remember what we
talked about last month there.

00:45:26.800 --> 00:45:31.060
[LAUGHS] I think that I love
to stay on this topic too,

00:45:31.060 --> 00:45:35.800
because I see, Nessa, you
had some great thoughts that

00:45:35.800 --> 00:45:37.460
tack on to this idea as well.

00:45:37.460 --> 00:45:39.805
I didn't know if you
wanted to jump in as well.

00:45:43.410 --> 00:45:46.010
And I don't mean to be
putting people on the spot.

00:45:46.010 --> 00:45:49.260
I love when people just
share ideas in the chat too.

00:45:49.260 --> 00:45:52.190
But Marlene, feel free to
jump in with your thoughts

00:45:52.190 --> 00:45:53.120
on the topic too.

00:45:56.478 --> 00:45:57.895
Sorry, which
thoughts are those?

00:45:57.895 --> 00:45:59.110
[LAUGHS]

00:45:59.110 --> 00:46:00.190
Well, no, I just see--

00:46:00.190 --> 00:46:02.350
I'm trying to read
at the same time

00:46:02.350 --> 00:46:04.810
the conversation happening
in the chat around this,

00:46:04.810 --> 00:46:08.050
like user experience
for the dashboards

00:46:08.050 --> 00:46:10.880
and thoughts that you and Nessa
are sharing back and forth.

00:46:10.880 --> 00:46:13.930
So just seeing if you
want to share that.

00:46:13.930 --> 00:46:16.388
I think-- so there's
two components,

00:46:16.388 --> 00:46:17.680
what you show and what you say.

00:46:17.680 --> 00:46:19.300
And Emi's hitting--

00:46:19.300 --> 00:46:20.740
I'm just so appreciating.

00:46:20.740 --> 00:46:22.270
I think I think these things.

00:46:22.270 --> 00:46:24.730
But hearing them out
loud from somebody else

00:46:24.730 --> 00:46:26.620
with an organization
that has their stuff

00:46:26.620 --> 00:46:28.238
way more together
than mine, that's

00:46:28.238 --> 00:46:29.530
not a knock on my organization.

00:46:29.530 --> 00:46:31.322
It's just 100 years
old, and they do things

00:46:31.322 --> 00:46:33.250
like 100-year-old organization.

00:46:33.250 --> 00:46:36.730
But the doing-- the saying
the things you need to say

00:46:36.730 --> 00:46:37.610
is very important.

00:46:37.610 --> 00:46:39.920
So Emi's hitting that
right on the head.

00:46:39.920 --> 00:46:41.620
I struggle with I
want to do everything

00:46:41.620 --> 00:46:42.593
and do everything well.

00:46:42.593 --> 00:46:44.260
And I had to realize
a couple years ago,

00:46:44.260 --> 00:46:45.940
I can't do everything well.

00:46:45.940 --> 00:46:48.250
So I have to lean
on people to help me

00:46:48.250 --> 00:46:49.550
with the visualization bit.

00:46:49.550 --> 00:46:51.340
So that was the bit
I put in the chat.

00:46:51.340 --> 00:46:53.800
So two people that
can really help you

00:46:53.800 --> 00:46:55.900
is usually somebody in
the marketing department

00:46:55.900 --> 00:46:58.990
who really have an eye
for user experience,

00:46:58.990 --> 00:47:01.870
how something's laid out
visually, and how it flows.

00:47:01.870 --> 00:47:04.293
I get that guy to look at it.

00:47:04.293 --> 00:47:06.460
It could be a woman in your
organization or somebody

00:47:06.460 --> 00:47:07.690
non-binary.

00:47:07.690 --> 00:47:10.960
But the marketing
guy, the UX guy,

00:47:10.960 --> 00:47:14.860
I ping him at least two,
three times during a project

00:47:14.860 --> 00:47:18.580
to make sure, does this
need major overhaul?

00:47:18.580 --> 00:47:19.810
Is this OK?

00:47:19.810 --> 00:47:22.240
Does it get the story across?

00:47:22.240 --> 00:47:24.490
And then I get somebody
with finance experience.

00:47:24.490 --> 00:47:26.320
Doesn't have to be
a bigwig of finance,

00:47:26.320 --> 00:47:29.170
even somebody who's just in
the accounting department

00:47:29.170 --> 00:47:30.405
or finance.

00:47:30.405 --> 00:47:31.780
They can be
different departments

00:47:31.780 --> 00:47:32.710
in your organization.

00:47:32.710 --> 00:47:36.670
But somebody who can see
the numbers beyond the data.

00:47:36.670 --> 00:47:38.620
So this trend, this
upward trend, well,

00:47:38.620 --> 00:47:40.120
what does that mean to you?

00:47:40.120 --> 00:47:41.950
Oh, well, yeah, I
could see that we might

00:47:41.950 --> 00:47:43.090
want to spend money there.

00:47:43.090 --> 00:47:44.830
Or we need to cut costs there.

00:47:44.830 --> 00:47:47.840
So if they can extrapolate that
easily from what you've done,

00:47:47.840 --> 00:47:49.750
I think you've done
more than half the job.

00:47:49.750 --> 00:47:51.220
The rest is just the speaking.

00:47:51.220 --> 00:47:53.860
And then, following on, I want
to say, Emi's kind of book

00:47:53.860 --> 00:47:55.790
of, keep it simple, stupid.

00:47:55.790 --> 00:47:56.290
Right?

00:47:56.290 --> 00:47:58.060
Just keep the bullet points.

00:47:58.060 --> 00:47:58.987
Or for those of you--

00:47:58.987 --> 00:48:00.070
I might date myself here--

00:48:00.070 --> 00:48:00.788
Dragnet, right?

00:48:00.788 --> 00:48:01.330
Facts, ma'am.

00:48:01.330 --> 00:48:02.300
Just the facts.

00:48:02.300 --> 00:48:04.900
So just giving the
facts and the takeaways,

00:48:04.900 --> 00:48:07.420
I think, really, really helped.

00:48:07.420 --> 00:48:09.130
So teaching a bit
of a master class

00:48:09.130 --> 00:48:10.750
here, Emi, like a Ted Talk.

00:48:10.750 --> 00:48:13.480
I kind of want to just bottle
you up and take you everywhere.

00:48:13.480 --> 00:48:15.100
Thanks for your insights.

00:48:15.100 --> 00:48:15.620
Oh, no.

00:48:15.620 --> 00:48:16.120
Thank you.

00:48:16.120 --> 00:48:16.480
Thank you.

00:48:16.480 --> 00:48:17.688
Thank you for the kind words.

00:48:17.688 --> 00:48:19.900
And you're right.

00:48:19.900 --> 00:48:21.040
Nobody knows everything.

00:48:21.040 --> 00:48:22.630
You need to check with people.

00:48:22.630 --> 00:48:24.340
Just be careful.

00:48:24.340 --> 00:48:26.710
Because you want
to get it perfect.

00:48:26.710 --> 00:48:28.720
And you want to
check with everyone.

00:48:28.720 --> 00:48:31.268
You might get a paralysis
because you're, well,

00:48:31.268 --> 00:48:32.810
I need to check with
the user design.

00:48:32.810 --> 00:48:33.893
I need to check with this.

00:48:33.893 --> 00:48:36.610
I need to check-- and then
you keep checking with things

00:48:36.610 --> 00:48:38.950
and trying to make sure
everything is perfect.

00:48:38.950 --> 00:48:41.500
And then you never get it out.

00:48:41.500 --> 00:48:44.020
So I used to have my--

00:48:44.020 --> 00:48:46.030
I was an entrepreneur
for a little bit,

00:48:46.030 --> 00:48:47.560
I had my own company.

00:48:47.560 --> 00:48:50.170
And I think that helped.

00:48:50.170 --> 00:48:54.550
Because when you're
an entrepreneur,

00:48:54.550 --> 00:48:55.520
you don't have money.

00:48:55.520 --> 00:48:58.030
You need to get things out
and start making money.

00:48:58.030 --> 00:49:01.300
So that pushed you to, well,
I need to get something out.

00:49:01.300 --> 00:49:02.720
I can bring the income.

00:49:02.720 --> 00:49:07.400
So think about the same as a
data person, a data scientist,

00:49:07.400 --> 00:49:09.610
you want to get something
out to start producing

00:49:09.610 --> 00:49:10.780
money for the company.

00:49:10.780 --> 00:49:12.490
You're going to make it better.

00:49:12.490 --> 00:49:15.580
But you need to have the due
diligence in terms of security

00:49:15.580 --> 00:49:17.410
and accuracy and all that.

00:49:17.410 --> 00:49:23.035
But think about, yeah, you
don't want to rush things,

00:49:23.035 --> 00:49:23.920
it's too much.

00:49:23.920 --> 00:49:26.350
But you got to get the money.

00:49:26.350 --> 00:49:28.030
So check with people.

00:49:28.030 --> 00:49:30.070
Make sure that everybody
thinks the same.

00:49:30.070 --> 00:49:32.200
But keep it moving.

00:49:32.200 --> 00:49:35.470
Prototype it, get it out,
ask for some feedback,

00:49:35.470 --> 00:49:40.200
and keep making it better.

00:49:40.200 --> 00:49:41.580
Thank you.

00:49:41.580 --> 00:49:43.920
AJ, I see you had asked
a question earlier

00:49:43.920 --> 00:49:46.020
on different tools.

00:49:46.020 --> 00:49:49.000
Do you want to jump in?

00:49:49.000 --> 00:49:49.500
Yeah.

00:49:49.500 --> 00:49:52.230
I was just curious though,
for your development process,

00:49:52.230 --> 00:49:54.330
whether you use a
traditional common bond

00:49:54.330 --> 00:49:58.680
way of producing content,
such as analytics dashboard,

00:49:58.680 --> 00:50:01.110
or you follow all
agile approach, where

00:50:01.110 --> 00:50:04.350
you treat one dashboard
as its own digital product

00:50:04.350 --> 00:50:09.102
and continuously every PI,
make improvements to it?

00:50:09.102 --> 00:50:10.560
Because I've seen
both in the past.

00:50:10.560 --> 00:50:11.810
They have their pros and cons.

00:50:11.810 --> 00:50:14.670
I was just curious if
you had opinions on what

00:50:14.670 --> 00:50:17.410
one works better than the other?

00:50:17.410 --> 00:50:18.190
Yeah.

00:50:18.190 --> 00:50:24.610
I think it's, at one point
we tried to go a full agile

00:50:24.610 --> 00:50:31.520
and follow all the
methodologies used by IT.

00:50:31.520 --> 00:50:33.100
I don't think that was the best.

00:50:33.100 --> 00:50:34.660
I think it has to be--

00:50:34.660 --> 00:50:37.960
we follow a [INAUDIBLE]
DM methodology.

00:50:37.960 --> 00:50:43.630
And but we realized that data
scientists and the teams,

00:50:43.630 --> 00:50:49.120
they don't like the daily
standards and the full agile

00:50:49.120 --> 00:50:49.990
concept.

00:50:49.990 --> 00:50:53.950
They need time to sit
down, think through

00:50:53.950 --> 00:50:56.690
the problem, work on that.

00:50:56.690 --> 00:51:02.590
And so, we give them that space
so they can focus and spend

00:51:02.590 --> 00:51:04.330
quality time on the project.

00:51:04.330 --> 00:51:08.380
We try not to bug you
a lot and protect them.

00:51:08.380 --> 00:51:14.710
So it's, again, [INAUDIBLE]
DM, a little bit of DevOps.

00:51:14.710 --> 00:51:20.530
Of course, when we implement the
solution, we work with type DM,

00:51:20.530 --> 00:51:25.330
we follow their methodologies,
their agile, DevOps,

00:51:25.330 --> 00:51:26.590
and all that.

00:51:26.590 --> 00:51:28.930
We use DataOps.

00:51:28.930 --> 00:51:34.300
We started implementing
DataOps for the data part.

00:51:34.300 --> 00:51:37.840
And now we have MLOps for
all the model monitoring

00:51:37.840 --> 00:51:39.560
once it's in production.

00:51:39.560 --> 00:51:45.760
So I think I will go with
this, building those parts,

00:51:45.760 --> 00:51:51.620
and giving them the
freedom to work together.

00:51:51.620 --> 00:51:54.820
And that has proven
to be the best.

00:51:54.820 --> 00:52:00.350
Don't try to go all
in with the agile

00:52:00.350 --> 00:52:03.400
and in traditional
IT technologies.

00:52:03.400 --> 00:52:07.090
Of course, the things that
probably they hate the most

00:52:07.090 --> 00:52:14.530
is all the waterfall and
PowerPoint status updates

00:52:14.530 --> 00:52:15.480
and things like that.

00:52:15.480 --> 00:52:17.560
They really don't like that.

00:52:17.560 --> 00:52:20.590
Of course, we need to share
with management updates

00:52:20.590 --> 00:52:21.860
and know what's going on.

00:52:21.860 --> 00:52:26.710
But we don't force
them, or we don't

00:52:26.710 --> 00:52:31.870
make them build this beautiful
PowerPoint presentation to keep

00:52:31.870 --> 00:52:33.640
management happy.

00:52:33.640 --> 00:52:35.900
It's a lot of--
part of my job is

00:52:35.900 --> 00:52:38.590
trying to protect them to give
them the freedom and the time

00:52:38.590 --> 00:52:41.740
to focus on what they do
best that is not PowerPoints,

00:52:41.740 --> 00:52:45.230
or having stand-ups
and things like that.

00:52:45.230 --> 00:52:49.612
But to sit down and
build algorithms.

00:52:52.350 --> 00:52:53.130
Thank you.

00:52:53.130 --> 00:52:56.850
I see you have a fellow
Nationwider on the call

00:52:56.850 --> 00:52:59.985
too, Francisco, do you want
to jump in with your question?

00:53:03.060 --> 00:53:03.630
Yeah.

00:53:03.630 --> 00:53:04.680
Can you hear me OK?

00:53:04.680 --> 00:53:05.040
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Yes.

00:53:05.040 --> 00:53:05.910
EMI ZAMBADA: Yeah.

00:53:05.910 --> 00:53:06.450
All right.

00:53:06.450 --> 00:53:07.590
Hi, Emi.

00:53:07.590 --> 00:53:10.830
My question was just
basic general advice

00:53:10.830 --> 00:53:14.220
for transitioning
into a data science

00:53:14.220 --> 00:53:17.250
and analytics-focused role,
specifically for someone

00:53:17.250 --> 00:53:20.520
coming from somewhere else.

00:53:20.520 --> 00:53:22.320
Personally, in my
experience, I'm going--

00:53:22.320 --> 00:53:25.320
I actually just found out
I'm moving towards that data

00:53:25.320 --> 00:53:29.400
and analytics role in a recent
team shift where I'm coming

00:53:29.400 --> 00:53:32.290
from operational support.

00:53:32.290 --> 00:53:34.200
And I don't really
have much experience

00:53:34.200 --> 00:53:37.200
within analytics
and data science

00:53:37.200 --> 00:53:40.770
studying operations in college,
where my internships were

00:53:40.770 --> 00:53:41.890
mostly focused on that.

00:53:41.890 --> 00:53:43.740
So I was wondering,
from your perspective

00:53:43.740 --> 00:53:48.360
as a leader, what advice and
insight do you have for someone

00:53:48.360 --> 00:53:50.790
who is brand new, like myself?

00:53:50.790 --> 00:53:51.750
Yeah.

00:53:51.750 --> 00:53:56.910
Don't try to be a data
scientist from scratch.

00:53:56.910 --> 00:54:01.320
It requires a lot of
expertise, a lot of knowledge,

00:54:01.320 --> 00:54:03.360
and experience, and education.

00:54:03.360 --> 00:54:05.650
A lot of people they go
and take an online course,

00:54:05.650 --> 00:54:06.360
which is great.

00:54:06.360 --> 00:54:09.450
Or they build up a
little toy project,

00:54:09.450 --> 00:54:12.323
and they want to go
into data scientist,

00:54:12.323 --> 00:54:14.490
and they don't get the job,
and they get frustrated.

00:54:14.490 --> 00:54:17.220
So start with something
data analytics,

00:54:17.220 --> 00:54:23.910
start with something
business intelligence,

00:54:23.910 --> 00:54:28.260
supporting an area with some
dashboard, some analysis,

00:54:28.260 --> 00:54:29.970
even if it's an Excel.

00:54:29.970 --> 00:54:33.000
Build those projects
that get you started.

00:54:33.000 --> 00:54:35.280
And then think about,
how much passion,

00:54:35.280 --> 00:54:36.990
or where do you want to go?

00:54:36.990 --> 00:54:41.040
If I think about myself, I was
passionate about the topic.

00:54:41.040 --> 00:54:44.460
I've been learning, always
trying to learn new things.

00:54:44.460 --> 00:54:47.520
But I knew myself
that I would not

00:54:47.520 --> 00:54:51.150
be able to compete with the data
scientists, people that have

00:54:51.150 --> 00:54:52.500
PhDs, they're mathematicians.

00:54:52.500 --> 00:54:54.120
They're really good
at what they do.

00:54:54.120 --> 00:54:56.580
If I would have gone
into that track,

00:54:56.580 --> 00:54:58.650
I would probably
have failed and not

00:54:58.650 --> 00:55:01.200
be as competitive and
as good as they are.

00:55:01.200 --> 00:55:03.090
But then I thought
about, well, what

00:55:03.090 --> 00:55:05.820
are the things that I think
I do best and the things

00:55:05.820 --> 00:55:08.010
that-- it's like you
have a new product.

00:55:08.010 --> 00:55:10.020
You go back to being
an entrepreneur.

00:55:10.020 --> 00:55:11.430
You are your own product.

00:55:11.430 --> 00:55:12.750
You are your own brand.

00:55:12.750 --> 00:55:15.030
And think about it, you
will go in the market

00:55:15.030 --> 00:55:17.940
and compete versus
Apple with the iPhone.

00:55:17.940 --> 00:55:19.890
You are not going to
make a good deal there.

00:55:19.890 --> 00:55:24.810
You are not going to be
able to beat the Apple

00:55:24.810 --> 00:55:26.880
and be better that--

00:55:26.880 --> 00:55:28.810
build something better
than the iPhone.

00:55:28.810 --> 00:55:31.230
So instead of that say,
but what is the guy-- what

00:55:31.230 --> 00:55:34.175
is that thing that is missing?

00:55:34.175 --> 00:55:35.550
And then they will
say, oh, well,

00:55:35.550 --> 00:55:37.800
there's not an app
for to do this.

00:55:37.800 --> 00:55:41.980
Then build that app that
will make an iPhone better.

00:55:41.980 --> 00:55:44.190
So think about that
in your organization

00:55:44.190 --> 00:55:45.960
or in the industry.

00:55:45.960 --> 00:55:48.330
And what is that data scientist?

00:55:48.330 --> 00:55:49.680
Yeah, we'll never have enough.

00:55:49.680 --> 00:55:52.050
But there are a lot of
good people, very smart.

00:55:52.050 --> 00:55:54.690
But maybe think about,
maybe in my organization,

00:55:54.690 --> 00:55:57.240
there are not a lot of
very good data engineers.

00:55:57.240 --> 00:55:58.320
And I like that.

00:55:58.320 --> 00:56:00.780
Maybe start being
a data engineer,

00:56:00.780 --> 00:56:02.310
because there is the need.

00:56:02.310 --> 00:56:03.570
There is the market need.

00:56:03.570 --> 00:56:06.060
Again, be yourself
a brand, a product.

00:56:06.060 --> 00:56:07.500
And I don't mean
it in a bad way.

00:56:07.500 --> 00:56:11.940
But be-- fill that need,
understand that need,

00:56:11.940 --> 00:56:15.280
and try to fulfill that need.

00:56:15.280 --> 00:56:20.575
And then continue
learning and continue

00:56:20.575 --> 00:56:24.340
filling out what is your career
and where do you want to go.

00:56:24.340 --> 00:56:26.580
So I hope that helps.

00:56:26.580 --> 00:56:27.900
Continue learning.

00:56:27.900 --> 00:56:30.360
Don't get desperate.

00:56:30.360 --> 00:56:35.270
But don't try to get
pushed to something

00:56:35.270 --> 00:56:39.420
that might not fit with
your career progression

00:56:39.420 --> 00:56:42.630
or what you can really
do in the short-term,

00:56:42.630 --> 00:56:44.340
and not get frustrated.

00:56:44.340 --> 00:56:51.850
There will be someone or a
position that might help you.

00:56:51.850 --> 00:56:52.350
Yes.

00:56:52.350 --> 00:56:55.470
Thank you so much.

00:56:55.470 --> 00:57:00.220
I think on this topic as well,
there is a kind of related.

00:57:00.220 --> 00:57:01.680
There was a Slido question.

00:57:01.680 --> 00:57:04.200
Is there anything that
you or your company

00:57:04.200 --> 00:57:06.330
looks for in new
data science hires,

00:57:06.330 --> 00:57:10.390
so on both the technical
and soft skills?

00:57:10.390 --> 00:57:11.080
Yeah.

00:57:11.080 --> 00:57:14.290
And I put it on the
chat, my LinkedIn,

00:57:14.290 --> 00:57:18.460
if anyone wants to
connect, happy to do so.

00:57:18.460 --> 00:57:22.480
I think there's
definitely the demand,

00:57:22.480 --> 00:57:26.290
the technical
expertise, the coding.

00:57:26.290 --> 00:57:32.590
But being very analytical,
like asking questions,

00:57:32.590 --> 00:57:36.520
be someone that cares
about the business,

00:57:36.520 --> 00:57:42.700
someone that is passionate about
that, that asks good questions,

00:57:42.700 --> 00:57:46.240
think through problem-solving,
think about the problems,

00:57:46.240 --> 00:57:50.360
and what is the thing that
we really need to solve.

00:57:50.360 --> 00:57:52.900
So I think it's a combination.

00:57:52.900 --> 00:57:55.550
And some people are better with
one thing, better at the other.

00:57:55.550 --> 00:57:57.850
So try to fit,
again, your profile

00:57:57.850 --> 00:58:01.690
to whatever the
spectrum that can be.

00:58:01.690 --> 00:58:05.470
If you are very into the
technical thing, the math,

00:58:05.470 --> 00:58:06.350
you can do that.

00:58:06.350 --> 00:58:10.850
But if you are more on the
analytical or the business

00:58:10.850 --> 00:58:13.620
translator, do that.

00:58:13.620 --> 00:58:16.830
But we are very rigorous
into data scientists.

00:58:16.830 --> 00:58:19.440
And we have a very
strict hiring process.

00:58:19.440 --> 00:58:24.800
So that involves a lot of
the technical expertise.

00:58:24.800 --> 00:58:27.260
And depending on if
you send [INAUDIBLE]

00:58:27.260 --> 00:58:31.760
vision, whatever that might
be, we have a specific process.

00:58:31.760 --> 00:58:34.460
But again, don't
feel discouraged

00:58:34.460 --> 00:58:35.870
if you don't have all of that.

00:58:35.870 --> 00:58:41.310
Maybe find that role
that fit you need.

00:58:41.310 --> 00:58:42.180
Thank you.

00:58:42.180 --> 00:58:43.890
I know we just hit
