00:59:07.990 --> 00:59:10.230
But the question was, with
so many data scientists,

00:59:10.230 --> 00:59:12.570
I assume a poly-lingual team.

00:59:12.570 --> 00:59:15.750
And you talked about all the
different languages people use.

00:59:15.750 --> 00:59:19.770
How have you navigated the R
Python dichotomy, especially

00:59:19.770 --> 00:59:24.260
with respect to
deployment solutions?

00:59:24.260 --> 00:59:24.770
Yeah.

00:59:24.770 --> 00:59:29.960
Our model factory
environment is suited

00:59:29.960 --> 00:59:33.660
for both and other languages.

00:59:33.660 --> 00:59:40.100
So everything we build, try
to be flexible with whatever

00:59:40.100 --> 00:59:41.660
programming language is used.

00:59:41.660 --> 00:59:43.940
Basically, R and Python.

00:59:43.940 --> 00:59:47.690
There's people that are
experts in R, others in Python.

00:59:47.690 --> 00:59:50.630
But a lot of that,
as an insurance

00:59:50.630 --> 00:59:54.320
company, financial services,
we're very heavy on R.

00:59:54.320 --> 00:59:57.920
But it's transitioning,
probably 70, 80, 20,

00:59:57.920 --> 01:00:00.710
I think we're 50/50 but
moving towards Python.

01:00:00.710 --> 01:00:03.650
So people are trying
to learn both.

01:00:03.650 --> 01:00:08.450
And they proven to be more
successful if they know both.

01:00:08.450 --> 01:00:13.320
So not easy and difficult
to have expectation

01:00:13.320 --> 01:00:14.480
to be an expert on both.

01:00:14.480 --> 01:00:18.010
But if you feel
expertise in one,

01:00:18.010 --> 01:00:21.220
but then know how to do a little
basic on the other language,

01:00:21.220 --> 01:00:23.035
that can be extremely powerful.

